  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to agriculture and farmland protection programs",
    "basePrintNo": "S4158",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4158-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§324 & 324-a, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4158\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to agriculture and farmland protection programs\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nWould include efforts to support transfer of farmland to beginning\nfarmers as part of local government farmland protection programs.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill would amend sections 324 and 324-a of the Agriculture and\nMarkets Law by amending county and municipal farmland protection\nprovisions to encourage efforts to support transfer of farmland to new\nowners, especially beginning farmers.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe most recent federal census of agriculture in 2007 found that the\naverage age of farmers in NYS is about 56, up from 50 in the early\n1980s. The U.S. Secretary of Agriculture has set a goal of recruiting\n100,000 new farmers across the country to replace those that are\nretiring or leaving.\n\nYoung people are increasingly interested in farming and are looking\nfor land they can farm. Many local governments, both counties and some\nmunicipalities, are interested in preserving farms and keeping land in\nproduction. These governments are authorized under State law to\ndevelop farmland protection plans. Often the focus of these plans is\non the land itself, identifying prime farmland and properties for\npurchase of development rights. This bill would encourage local\ngovernment to also include planning efforts to support the transfer of\nfarmland to young and beginning farmers as part of the farmland\nprotection program and also as part of the comprehensive plan\ndeveloped by counties and municipalities.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2014: Passed Assembly.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
